6 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067990 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067990 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067990 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1045cfc3-db82-5ebf-9a58-ef4781fcdd16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067991 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067991 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067991 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "db1b76bd-cc4e-5494-bb2c-0ef5978d995e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067992 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067992 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067992 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eccebac7-1cc2-5ac8-8586-f1b4ba12efe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067993 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03eae620-0a72-5ac8-8c06-ed97d97ba78a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067994 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0af28cfe-55f0-5316-a265-32fa7f9b5716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067995 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067995 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067995 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42d3994d-6cc2-58e9-94f1-8aa6404ab537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068624 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068624 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $2x < 10\\,?$\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd57c7f2-87b5-55a0-9237-1ef814f59898" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068624 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 0 & <10 \\\\ 0 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=0$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2 & <10 \\\\ 4 & <10\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=2$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}5}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot x & <10 \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & <10 \\\\ 10 & <10\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=5$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=5$ **does not satisfy** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068625 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068625 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following values of $x$ satisfy the inequality $1 \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}-1 < 3\\,?$\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=4$\n\nIII. $x=10$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74587421-d1ad-550e-a30d-4d4768588d4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068625 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute each value into the given inequality and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}0}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{0}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 0-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le -1<3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=0$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=0$ **does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}4}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{4}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 1<3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=4$ into the inequality leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=4$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}x}={\\color{blue}10}$ into the given inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le \\frac{x}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le \\frac{10}{2}-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 5-1<3 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4<3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $x=10$ into the inequality leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $x=10$ **does not satisfy** this inequality." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068626 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068626 #edb/ref 1071 "The value $a = 2$ satisfies which of the following inequalities?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{a}{2} + 2 > 5$\n\nII. $1 \\leq 2a < 6$\n\nIII. $3a - 1 < 10$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb564b00-2d04-556a-a662-7b92e21ec3d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068626 #edb/ref 1074 "We substitute the given value into each of the inequalities and check to see if the resulting statement is true or false.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}2}$ into the first inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{2}+2 & >5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{2}+2 & >5 \\\\ 1+2 & >5 \\\\ 3 & >5\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **false** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a = 2$ ** does not satisfy** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a}={\\color{blue}2}$ into the second inequality. We get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\le 2a<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2\\cdot a<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 2\\cdot 2<6 \\\\ 1 & \\le 4<6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting $a = 2$ leads to a **true** statement. \n\nTherefore, $a = 2$ **satisfies** this inequality.\n\n- We substitute the value ${\\color{blue}a} = {\\color{blue}